<!--
  The typed coordination engine, as a `dotnet tool` (ADR-0034 Phase 3a).

  THE FLIP HAS SINCE HAPPENED (ADR-0040 Phase D COMPLETE). This tool is no longer packaged to *shadow*
  bash — it IS the coordination client. `scripts/fsgg-coord` is now the §4.4 shim that execs this
  command, the ~7,000-line bash monolith is deleted, and the shadow, the `engine=` mode flag, and the
  fleet-divergence ledger are retired. The history below is why it was packaged BEFORE the flip; it is
  kept as the record of that ordering.

  WHY IT WAS PACKAGED FIRST, AND WHY THAT WAS *NOT* THE FLIP.

  Phase 2 shipped the shadow: both engines decide, bash's answer is returned, the disagreement is
  logged, and bash stays authoritative until that log is clean. Phase 2's exit criterion is "zero
  divergence across the LIVE FLEET for three consecutive days" — and it could never be met, because
  the engine existed only where somebody had BUILT it, which is `.github` and nowhere else. Fleet
  evidence needs the engine distributed; distribution was scheduled for Phase 3; Phase 3 was gated on
  Phase 2 exiting. The clock could never start (.github#617).

  So publishing came BEFORE flipping, and it was the only order in which the flip's evidence could
  exist. Bash stayed authoritative through Phase 3a. The single thing packaging changed was that a
  worker in a receiver repo now HAD an engine to shadow with.

  IDENTITY IS FOREVER (ADR-0003; "package identity == product identity, never rename in place"):

    PackageId        FS.GG.Coord.Cli     — the name ADR-0034 §4.1 gives it, and the right one for what
                                           it BECOMES: at the flip this is the coordination client.
    ToolCommandName  fsgg-coord-engine   — and it never changes either. At the flip, the `kit:` row
                                           `scripts/fsgg-coord` becomes a shim that EXECS this command,
                                           so the command name never has to be renamed and no tool
                                           manifest ever has to be rewritten.

  STABLE, NOT -preview. The org promoted its last `-preview` producer in July, and the shared Renovate
  preset now pins to stable (`ignoreUnstable: true`). A `-preview` tool would be INVISIBLE to Renovate
  — and Renovate-invisibility is one of the stated reasons ADR-0034 §4.4 rejected the self-contained
  binary. Shipping a tool the update fabric cannot see would defeat the point of shipping it.

  `.github` builds this FROM SOURCE and never restores it from the feed. That asymmetry is what breaks
  the publish cycle: a broken feed cannot stop the coordination tool being fixed, because the repo that
  fixes it does not depend on the feed. Only RECEIVERS consume the package.
-->
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RollForward>Major</RollForward>

    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackAsTool>true</PackAsTool>
    <ToolCommandName>fsgg-coord-engine</ToolCommandName>
    <PackageId>FS.GG.Coord.Cli</PackageId>
    <!-- 0.23.0 is a MINOR on the 0.x line: the `driver` events/cursor flags (.github#2135) are new
         caller-visible CLI vocabulary — a flag pair, a help entry, a render mode, and a durable cursor
         file format that 0.22.1 did not have. Diffing src/FS.GG.Coord.Cli/Options.fs between
         coord-engine/v0.22.1 and HEAD is non-empty (Events/CursorFile flags, spellings, applicability),
         so PATCH is not defensible even though Protocol.fs (the exit-code wire surface) is untouched;
         this repository's 0.x policy treats new CLI vocabulary as the caller-observable-change signal
         whenever there is no Major line to take. The 0.22.1 PATCH rationale follows as historical context. -->
    <!-- 0.22.1 is a PATCH on the 0.x line: #2308 bounds empty claim-marker reads so an
         empty response is refused rather than treated as a known-unclaimed item. No command,
         flag, result, or exit-code vocabulary changes; the fix is internal fail-closed behavior.
         The 0.22.0 MINOR rationale follows as historical context. -->
    <!-- 0.22.0 is a MINOR on the 0.x line: three wire-surface commits since the 0.21.1 release
         add the explicit delivery-route receipt boundary and typed intake transaction surface, while
         #2300 and #2313 repair scheduler/reconcile read amplification. Receivers gain new command and
         result vocabulary, so a patch would understate the caller-observable change. The remainder of
         this historical note records the earlier 0.16.0 rationale. -->
    <!-- 0.15.0 to 0.16.0 is a MINOR, and under the repository's 0.x SemVer policy that is also the
         caller-observable-change signal: there is no 1.x major available to take. Eight merged engine
         commits ship (.github#1951); #1900's squash commit consolidates four reviewed input commits.
         The cut includes a Protocol.fs wire-surface change and several independent changes
         to scheduler, claim, lint, follow-up, and done-boundary behavior:

           * #1915 (0b181f2) makes `lint` name consolidation candidates.
           * #1921 (2065040) makes BLOCKER-CLEARED respect an in-flight implementation.
           * #1901 (9d7c1a2) adds the ordered Severity ranking axis.
           * #1942 (338e9aa) corrects KIT DIGEST claim advice for skill files.
           * #1946 (5a93a3b) excludes decision items from the worker queue.
           * #1948 (4f3ffa6) derives skill views from receiver declarations.
           * #1949 (91645e2) fails closed when a marker scan is incomplete.
           * #1900/#1952 (de03135, squashing f9b067c, 7d9927e, a8466c1, and 0b722c9) adds follow-up
             audit/reconciliation, makes `done` audit residual promises, and adds the durable disposition
             boundary.

         Patch is not defensible: receivers gain command vocabulary, result vocabulary, refusal paths,
         rank behavior, and terminal behavior. #1892 (secondary-rate-limit exit contract) and #1857
         (mid-item session rotation) remain explicitly unresolved residue and are not part of this cut.

         This property is the RELEASE ACT (see scripts/check-engine-freshness.py): the engine's version
         moves only at release time, by hand, and release-coord-engine.yml evaluates it with
         `dotnet msbuild -getProperty:Version` and refuses any `coord-engine/v[version]` tag that
         disagrees. The tag is a coherence check against this line and never its source.

         0.23.0 is now the LAST independent literal this project declares (.github#2402). FS.GG.Kit,
         FS.GG.Drivers and this engine are a coherent set: one MSBuild property,
         $(FsggCoherentSetVersion) (Directory.Build.props), carries all three <Version> elements from
         here on, so a bump to one moves all three and the three cannot diverge from each other again.
         KEEP THIS PROPERTY IN STEP WITH THE ONE AT LINE ~127 (PackageReleaseNotes) EXACTLY AS BEFORE —
         that requirement (.github#1762) is unaffected by where the literal now lives; only the source
         of the literal moved. -->
    <Version>$(FsggCoherentSetVersion)</Version>
    <AssemblyName>fsgg-coord-engine</AssemblyName>

    <Description>FS-GG typed coordination engine — the schedulability model behind `fsgg-coord` as one total function over a board-state snapshot, plus the typed IO layer that drives it. It IS the coordination tool (ADR-0034/ADR-0040): the `fsgg-coord` shim resolves and execs it; there is no bash left to shadow.</Description>
    <Authors>FS-GG</Authors>
    <Company>FS-GG</Company>
    <PackageTags>fsgg;coordination;scheduler;dotnet-tool;fsharp</PackageTags>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/FS-GG/.github</RepositoryUrl>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/FS-GG/.github/tree/main/src/FS.GG.Coord.Cli</PackageProjectUrl>

<!-- THE RELEASE NOTES ARE WHERE THE BREAK IS ANNOUNCED, and that is the point of them here.
         nuget.org renders this on the package listing and `dotnet tool` consumers read it there. A
         changelog that said only "fixes" while a previously-accepted flag started exiting 1 would be
         the #266 shape in its purest form: the org knowing a thing, and the consumer discovering it
         from a broken script. Criterion 5 of .github#1596 asks for exactly this.

         KEEP THIS PROPERTY IN STEP WITH <Version> ABOVE. The 0.13.0 cut moved <Version> and left these
         notes reading "0.12.0 — BREAKING...", so the nuget.org listing for 0.13.0 announced the PREVIOUS
         release's break and none of its own seven commits. Nothing gates the two against each other
         (.github#1762). A consumer who reads the listing and finds the wrong release described has been
         failed by it exactly as much as one who finds nothing.

         THIS FIELD IS BOUNDED, AND ITS SHAPE IS LOAD-BEARING (.github#2579). It is exactly three
         parts, in this order: THIS release's entry, then $(FsggStandingAdvisories), then the pointer
         to the served history. It does NOT accumulate prior entries — that is what crossed
         nuget.org's 35,000-character limit and left FS.GG.Coord.Cli 0.52.0 published on the org feed
         and absent from nuget.org. The first token must remain the version (#1762), so the current
         entry stays first and the advisories are referenced, never prepended.

         WHEN YOU CUT THE NEXT RELEASE: REPLACE the entry below with the new version's, do not
         prepend to it. Leave the advisory reference and the pointer where they are. If this cut
         creates a new permanent partial set, add it to $(FsggStandingAdvisories) — that property is
         the only part of this field that persists across cuts, deliberately, and
         scripts/check-engine-release-notes.py refuses a listing that drops the reference or empties
         it. -->
    <FsggStandingAdvisories>STANDING ADVISORIES — these are NOT this release's notes, and they do not age out.

Two published FS.GG.Coord.Cli versions must never be adopted. Their own listings are immutable and
cannot be corrected, so the NEWEST listing is the only channel that can say so — and an unpinned
`dotnet tool install` resolves the newest version, which makes this the entry a consumer reads.
They live in their own MSBuild property (`FsggStandingAdvisories`) rather than inside the release
narrative below, so that trimming the narrative and deleting a warning are different edits
(.github#2579). `scripts/check-engine-release-notes.py` refuses a listing that stops referencing
this property or empties it; 4fccc76d deleted these warnings once while that gate was green.

DO NOT ADOPT 0.50.1. It is a permanent two-of-three set. The 0.50.1 cut published `FS.GG.Kit` and
`FS.GG.Drivers` to both feeds and was then REFUSED for this package by `check-engine-release-notes`
(`.github#1762`), which correctly flagged that the release notes still began with `0.50.0`. Both
sibling packages were already immutable at 0.50.1 and `.github#1772`'s sibling-tag precondition
requires all three tags to name the SAME commit, so 0.50.1 could not be completed from a corrected
tree. `FS.GG.Kit 0.50.1` and `FS.GG.Drivers 0.50.1` should not be adopted on their own; adopt
0.50.2, which is the whole set cut from one commit.

DO NOT ADOPT 0.50.5. It is a permanent two-of-three set, the same shape and the same cause as
0.50.1. `FS.GG.Kit 0.50.5` and `FS.GG.Drivers 0.50.5` published to both feeds; this package's
publish was then REFUSED by `check-engine-release-notes` because the notes still began with
`0.50.4` while `Version` had moved to `0.50.5`. Correcting that needs a new commit, and the
sibling-tag precondition requires all three tags at the SAME commit, so 0.50.5 could not be
finished from a corrected tree — re-cutting it is the re-pack divergence `.github#2240` documents.
Adopt 0.50.6.

DO NOT ADOPT 0.52.0. It is a different permanent partial set: `FS.GG.Kit` and `FS.GG.Drivers`
published to both feeds, and `FS.GG.Coord.Cli` published to the org feed before nuget.org rejected
its 37,279-character `PackageReleaseNotes`. NuGet versions are immutable and re-packing corrected
bytes under 0.52.0 would make the same version identify different artifacts. Adopt 0.53.0, the next
coherent set cut from corrected source. All three partial sets are recorded in
`registry/dependencies.yml`.</FsggStandingAdvisories>

    <PackageReleaseNotes>0.59.0 — five receiver-visible defect fixes, and the packed review/finding contracts that 0.58.1 was tagged for and never shipped.

WHAT MOVED, AND WHY IT IS A MINOR. Measured against 0.58.0, the newest version any consumer can actually
restore. Two things a receiver can observe move, and either alone would carry the line.

First, five defect fixes change what the tool does for you: `delivery` reports a pending check as a CHECK
rather than a review problem (.github#2575); a granted successor critic can record its verdict
(.github#2662); `take` walks its own ranked candidates after a lost claim CAS instead of giving up
(.github#2683); only LIVE claims count as implementer slots (.github#2678); and no inventory is no longer
treated as an empty inventory on any route (.github#2694).

Second, the packed `pnext-item` skill gains a new reviewer protocol boundary — the finding-packet contract
and the independent-review consolidation — which is the same shape the 0.57.0 cut took a minor for.

WHAT DID NOT MOVE, STATED SO THE LINE IS NOT MIS-READ. The `fsgg-coord-engine` wire surface is unchanged:
no verb, flag, help entry, render name, result name or exit code moved, and `Protocol.fs` is byte-identical
across this span. This release also carries a new core module, `Operation` (.github#2311) — but it is
INTERNAL TO THE TOOL and reaches no consumer: `FS.GG.Coord.Core` is not a package, this package is a
dotnet tool whose payload is invoked rather than referenced, and nothing calls that module yet. It is
recorded here for completeness, and it is not why this is a minor.

WHAT SHIPS IN THE KIT AT THIS VERSION. The finding-packet contract and the independent-review
consolidation in `pnext-item`, and the delivery-channel and skill-registry projections that moved with
them. These were tagged as 0.58.1 and never published, so 0.58.0 is the last kit any receiver has.

0.58.1 DOES NOT EXIST ON EITHER FEED AND IS NOT A VERSION TO WAIT FOR. Its three tags were pushed at one
commit and every release workflow then failed, because the draft release they consume had never been
prepared (.github#2654). No `FS.GG.Kit`, `FS.GG.Drivers` or `FS.GG.Coord.Cli` 0.58.1 was ever published to
the org feed or to nuget.org — so unlike 0.50.1, 0.50.5 and 0.52.0 below there is nothing to avoid
adopting. Tags are immutable (.github#1772), so 0.58.1 is skipped rather than completed, and this is the
cut that carries its content.

WHY THIS PACKAGE MOVES WITH THE KIT. The three packages resolve one scalar (`FsggCoherentSetVersion`,
`Directory.Build.props`) by construction (.github#2402), so all three ship at this same version.

$(FsggStandingAdvisories)

FULL PER-VERSION HISTORY IS SERVED BY THE REGISTRY, NOT REPEATED HERE (`.github#2579`). Every
published version's own notes remain readable, permanently, on that version's own listing at
https://www.nuget.org/packages/FS.GG.Coord.Cli — and each cut's evidence is recorded in
`registry/dependencies.yml` and `registry/CHANGELOG.md` at https://github.com/FS-GG/.github.

The release notes remain bounded: previous-version history stays on the registry listings and only
the standing safety advisories carry forward.
</PackageReleaseNotes>

    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!-- BUILD REPRODUCIBILITY, AND THE ROUTE DECISION .github#2688 ASKED FOR (its AC1).

         #2688 measured that two clean packs of one unchanged tree produce different
         `tools/net10.0/any/fsgg-coord-engine.dll` bytes, and offered two routes: make the build
         reproducible, or make the release saga's payload comparison tolerate the divergence. Taking
         the measurement further splits the defect in two, and the two halves have OPPOSITE answers.
         Both are recorded here because this is the file #2688 declared, and because the next person
         to meet an unstable `payloadSha256` will look for the setting before the reasoning.

         HALF ONE — THE CHECKOUT PATH — IS FIXED HERE, COMPLETELY AND DETERMINISTICALLY.
         SIX packed entries embedded the absolute directory the package happened to be built in:
         `FS.GG.Coord.Core`, `FS.GG.Coord.GitHub` and `fsgg-coord-engine`, each as `.dll` and `.pdb`.
         `DeterministicSourcePaths` maps them to the `/_/` source root, so the packed bytes stop
         depending on WHERE the tree was checked out. This is not cosmetic: it is the precondition for
         `payloadSha256` to be comparable ACROSS machines at all, which is what .github#2428 needs to
         compare a locally packed artifact against what a feed already serves, and what made three
         agents on #2688 report three different `payload_id` values for one tree. It is set on all
         three coord projects, not this one alone — fixing only this project would leave two of the six
         entries still path-bound and the package still not path-independent.

         HALF TWO — THE F# CLOSURE-NAME RACE — CANNOT BE FIXED FROM BUILD CONFIGURATION, AND THAT IS
         MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. The compiler disambiguates same-named generated closures with a
         `-N` suffix from a shared counter, and which of two `contains@1` closures gets `-1` is decided
         by a race: 12 of 64 clean compiles of one unchanged tree at ONE path diverged, with the rate
         rising with machine load (0/8, 3/24, 9/40 at compile concurrency 1, 6 and 10). Every lever was
         tried and every one is refuted, so DO NOT REACH FOR THEM AGAIN. (An XML comment may not contain
         a double hyphen, so compiler flags are named below without their leading dashes — the same
         convention the org build config states about itself.)

           * `<ParallelCompilation>` is INERT TWICE OVER. The F# SDK defaults it to `true` and then
             passes `ParallelCompilation="@(ParallelCompilation)"` — an ITEM reference to a PROPERTY —
             to the `Fsc` task (`Microsoft.FSharp.Targets:384`, SDK 10.0.302), so nothing reaches the
             compiler; and fsc refuses the flag it would have emitted anyway, with
             `FS0075: The command-line option 'parallelcompilation' is for test purposes only`.
             Setting this property changes nothing and proves nothing.
           * `test:ParallelOff` IS accepted by fsc and does NOT fix it: 7 of 40 compiles diverged,
             indistinguishable from the 9 of 40 baseline arm run beside it.
           * `crossoptimize` turned off makes it WORSE (16 of 40) and grows the closure inventory.
           * fsc 10.0.302 has NO other lever: its entire public option surface carries no concurrency
             control, and `ParallelOff`, `ParallelIlxGen` and `ParallelOptimization` are the only
             `test:` values it accepts in that family.
           * `<Deterministic>` is already on (`deterministic+` is on the real command line) and is
             NOT this lever. It is working: the MVID moves only BECAUSE the content moved.

         The only thing measured to suppress the race is `DOTNET_PROCESSOR_COUNT` (0 of 40 divergent
         at 1 and at 2, under the load where the baseline gave 9 of 40) — a PROCESS ENVIRONMENT
         VARIABLE that no MSBuild project file can set for the `Fsc` task. It is deliberately NOT
         wired in here: it would be a suppression rather than a guarantee, it would serialise every
         build that inherited it, and it cannot travel with the package.

         SO ROUTE TWO IS ALSO REFUSED, AND NOT MERELY DISPREFERRED. `scripts/release-saga.py`'s
         `normalized_relationships` docstring already states — in code merged and independently
         reviewed under .github#2664 — that hiding this divergence would be the fail-open
         .github#2240 and .github#2428 exist to prevent. This item does not reverse that. What it adds
         is the BOUND: across 144 compiles in five configurations the disambiguated-symbol MULTISET was
         identical every time (473 symbols, set difference empty) and only the ORDER of two of them
         moved, so a divergence outside that shape is a genuinely different tree and must still fail
         closed.

         `scripts/check-engine-build-determinism.py` enforces half one on the packed artifact, and
         `tests/engine-build-determinism/` shows it red on a build that omits this property. -->
    <DeterministicSourcePaths>true</DeterministicSourcePaths>

    <!-- RestorePackagesWithLockFile, RestoreLockedMode and DisableImplicitLibraryPacksFolder (ADR-0006,
         ADR-0032) are INHERITED from the root Directory.Build.props, which imports the org-shared
         config this repo authors and ships. Every project here used to set them by hand because
         .github did not consume its own build config (#609). It does now. -->

    <!-- The engine is invoked once per scheduling call, on every worker, in a fan-out. Startup cost
         is the whole of its runtime cost, so it takes no dependency it does not need. No
         Spectre.Console here: JSON is the contract, and the text projection is a handful of printfn. -->
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="Json.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Json.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Snapshot.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Snapshot.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Options.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Options.fs" />
    <Compile Include="DeliveryApplication.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="DeliveryApplication.fs" />
    <Compile Include="ReviewApplication.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="ReviewApplication.fs" />
    <Compile Include="CycleLedgerApplication.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="CycleLedgerApplication.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Identity.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Identity.fs" />
    <Compile Include="RefParsing.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="RefParsing.fs" />
    <Compile Include="FollowupAudit.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="FollowupAudit.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Chores.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Chores.fs" />
    <Compile Include="KitDigest.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="KitDigest.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Render.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Render.fs" />
    <Compile Include="ReadyApplication.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="ReadyApplication.fs" />
    <Compile Include="LintApplication.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="LintApplication.fs" />
    <Compile Include="BoardFactsApplication.fs" />
    <!-- AFTER `Client.fs`, deliberately: the follow-up queue reuses `parseRefIn` and the exit constants
         rather than growing a second answer to "what is a ref?" or "what does an empty look exit?" (#485).
         It is a LOCAL verb — no token, no board — and depends on `Client` for those two things only. -->
    <Compile Include="Followups.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Followups.fs" />
    <Compile Include="IntakeApplication.fs" />
    <Compile Include="DeliveryRouteApplication.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="DeliveryRouteApplication.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Client.fs" />
    <Compile Include="SemanticDiffApplication.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Program.fs" />
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <!-- Provider validator authority travels with the engine binary. Artifact roots are caller data;
         they must never select executable code (#2133 repair-phase round 1). The pinned byte digests
         in CycleLedgerApplication make an unreviewed validator replacement fail closed. -->
    <None Include="../../.agents/skills/work-roadmap/scripts/validate-critique-state.py"
          Link="provider-validators/validate-critique-state.py"
          CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <None Include="../../.agents/skills/work-roadmap/scripts/validate-feedback-state.py"
          Link="provider-validators/validate-feedback-state.py"
          CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../FS.GG.Coord.Core/FS.GG.Coord.Core.fsproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../FS.GG.Coord.GitHub/FS.GG.Coord.GitHub.fsproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="FSharp.Core" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
